<h1>Packages</h1>
<p>Packages are reusable, versioned building blocks: Lua gameplay modules and/or assets. Everything in the <a href="{{ path('catalog') }}">DemiEngine package catalog</a> is free. Games install packages into the project; the game never contacts a registry at runtime.</p>

<h2>What a package contains</h2>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "name": "demi.gameplay.health",
  "version": "1.1.0",
  "engine": ">=0.1.0",
  "dependencies": { "demi.gameplay.events": "^1.0.0" },
  "public_modules": ["demi.gameplay.health"],
  "files": [
    "README.md",
    "scripts/demi/gameplay/health.lua",
    "tests/health_test.lua"
  ]
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Package name</strong> is the installation identity.</li>
  <li><strong>Public modules</strong> are the import surface — one package can export several.</li>
  <li><strong>Files</strong> (with <code>asset_manifests</code> and <code>engine_extensions</code> subsets) are the audited content list.</li>
  <li>Never import another package's private module path.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Install packages</h2>
<pre><code>cd games/my_game
demi package add demi.gameplay.health@1.1.0
demi package add kenney.textures.prototype@1.0.0
demi package install
demi package list
demi package outdated</code></pre>
<p>Project <code>packages</code> holds constraints; <code>demi.packages.lock.json</code> pins resolved versions. Installs land in <code>.demi/packages</code> (derived data — do not commit or hand-edit).</p>
<p>Cook reads only the lockfile and <code>.demi/packages</code>. Package asset IDs merge into the shared registry without copying.</p>

<h2>Use package modules</h2>
<pre><code>local GameplayEvents = require("demi.gameplay.events")
local Health = require("demi.gameplay.health")

local health = Health.new(GameplayEvents.new())
health:add("player", 100)
health:damage({ target = "player", amount = 15 })
print(health:get("player").current)</code></pre>
<p>Installed package modules follow the same explicit-require rules as engine services. <code>Health.new</code> takes the events bus as its first argument, so gameplay systems can subscribe to <code>damage_applied</code>, <code>health_changed</code>, and <code>entity_defeated</code>. Editor stubs come from the package's public module metadata.</p>

