<h2>Test a package</h2>
<pre><code>demi package test packages/my_package
demi package test packages/my_package --format json</code></pre>
<p>Declared Lua tests run in the same runtime used by games.</p>

<h2>Publish a package</h2>
<ol>
  <li>List the package's files, including its root <code>README.md</code>, in <code>demi.package.json</code>. Run its tests before publishing.</li>
  <li>Build a local registry archive: <code>demi package publish path/to/package --registry /path/to/staging-registry</code>. The archive is written below <code>packages/&lt;name&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/package.demipkg</code> in that registry.</li>
  <li>Upload the archive and listing metadata using the <a href="{{ path('publishing') }}">publishing page</a> or <code>python3 tools/package-store/bin/publish.py package.demipkg metadata.json</code>. Publishing requires the private registry key.</li>
</ol>
<p>Releases are immutable. Listing metadata (title, description, tags, images) can be updated later without touching archive bytes.</p>
<p>The package overview displays the README bundled in the published archive. Deploying the website or editing a repository README does not update a published package. Bump the package version and publish a new archive when its code, manifest, or README changes. Publish dependencies before packages that require them.</p>

<h2>Design rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Gameplay rules belong in Lua or reusable packages, so the engine stays a general mechanism.</li>
  <li>Package dependencies use manifests and locks with declared visibility.</li>
  <li>Keep module names stable across releases so games do not need rewrites on every update.</li>
  <li>When a public API changes, update examples, docs, tests, and stubs together.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reusable character needs</h2>
<p><a href="/packages/demi.gameplay.needs">Character Needs</a> supplies named bounded values, time-based drain/recovery rates and separate low/recovery thresholds. It works without scene, physics or HUD dependencies. Games decide when to rest, what food costs and what happens on depletion. The Colony Builder example uses it for stamina, nutrition and suit oxygen.</p>
<pre><code>local Needs = require("demi.gameplay.needs")
local needs = Needs.new({
  stamina = { value = 80, low = 25, recovered = 90 },
})
needs:update(dt, { stamina = -0.4 })
if needs:urgent() then
  -- Ask your game's task scheduler for a break.
end</code></pre>
